.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt NQX5SELF 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqx5self ,
.Nm nqx5kem ,
.Nm nqx5info
.Nd X.509 certificates for noqtis keys
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fn nqx5self "nqsalg alg" "const uint8_t *sk" "size_t sklen" "const uint8_t *pk" "size_t pklen" "const char *cn" "const char *email" "int64_t nb" "int64_t na" "uint8_t *out" "size_t cap" "size_t *outlen"
.Ft int
.Fn nqx5kem "nqsalg ialg" "const uint8_t *isk" "size_t isklen" "const uint8_t *icert" "size_t icertlen" "nqkalg alg" "const uint8_t *pk" "size_t pklen" "int64_t nb" "int64_t na" "uint8_t *out" "size_t cap" "size_t *outlen"
.Ft int
.Fn nqx5info "const uint8_t *cert" "size_t n" "nqx5f *f"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions make and read X.509 certificates (RFC 5280), in DER.
.Pp
.Fn nqx5self
makes a self-signed certificate for the signature key pair
.Fa sk ,
.Fa pk
of
.Fa alg :
ML-DSA (RFC 9881), SLH-DSA (RFC 9909) or Ed25519 (RFC 8410).
Its subject is the common name
.Fa cn
(as
.Xr nqcard 3
takes a name) with, when
.Fa email
is not
.Dv NULL ,
that address as the rfc822Name of its subjectAltName.
It is valid from
.Fa nb
to
.Fa na
(seconds since 1970, UTC), signs mail, and is a certification authority
of path length 0.
.Pp
.Fn nqx5kem
makes a certificate for the ML-KEM public key
.Fa pk
of
.Fa alg
(RFC 9935), issued by the holder of the certificate
.Fa icert
with its secret key
.Fa isk
of
.Fa ialg ,
under the name and address of
.Fa icert .
.Pp
Both write a version 3 certificate whose fields are:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
a serial number of 16 random bytes, positive and with its second bit
set, so always 16 bytes long;
.It
the validity as UTCTime through 2049 and GeneralizedTime from 2050
(RFC 5280 section 4.1.2.5);
.It
the issuer and subject as the common name alone (for
.Fn nqx5kem
both are those of
.Fa icert ) ,
and the key as a SubjectPublicKeyInfo with no parameters;
.It
basicConstraints, critical, cA TRUE and pathLenConstraint 0
.Pf ( Fn nqx5self
only);
.It
keyUsage, critical: digitalSignature, nonRepudiation and keyCertSign
for
.Fn nqx5self ,
keyEncipherment alone for
.Fn nqx5kem ;
.It
extKeyUsage emailProtection, not critical;
.It
subjectKeyIdentifier, the first 20 bytes of the SHA-256 of the public
key (RFC 7093 section 2, method 1), and for
.Fn nqx5kem
an authorityKeyIdentifier, the same of the issuer's key;
.It
subjectAltName with the rfc822Name, when there is an address.
.El
.Pp
The signature is the pure form of the algorithm, with the empty context,
over the DER of the TBSCertificate; a certificate is verified with the
key it was signed by before it is written.
.Pp
.Fn nqx5info
reads a certificate into
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct {
  int kem;          /* 1: an ML-KEM key (alg an nqkalg) */
  int alg;          /* nqsalg or nqkalg; 0 for another kind */
  uint8_t fp[32];   /* SHA-256 of the certificate */
  int64_t nb, na;   /* valid from, to */
  int ca;
  char name[256];   /* the common name, "" when none */
  char email[255];  /* the first rfc822Name, "" when none */
  size_t pkoff;     /* the public key: where it is in the */
  size_t pklen;     /* certificate, and its length */
} nqx5f;
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa out
holds
.Fa cap
bytes and
.Fa outlen
gets the size.
A certificate nobody else signed proves nothing by itself: its holder
gives it to those who are to trust the key.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Dv NQOK
on success.
.Dv NQEARG
for a
.Dv NULL
pointer, a name or address
.Xr nqcard 3
would refuse, a key of the wrong length,
.Fa na
not after
.Fa nb ,
.Fa nb
before 1970 or a time after 9999,
or a key pair or issuer that does not match;
.Dv NQENOSUP
for an algorithm with no standard certificate (the hybrids, HQC);
.Dv NQEDEC
for a certificate that is not one;
.Dv NQEBUF
when
.Fa out
is too small, with the size in
.Fa outlen .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-cert 1 ,
.Xr noqtis 3 ,
.Xr nqcmssign 3 ,
.Xr nqerr 3
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 5280, RFC 8410, RFC 9881, RFC 9909, RFC 9935, RFC 7093.
